			"url": "https://tldr.nettime.org/@lowtech/115901197451058825",
			"author": {
				"name": "James Wallbank",
				"url": "https://tldr.nettime.org/@lowtech",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2026-01-15T21:18:43.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Hot take (from someone who's been a passionate supporter of open licenses, and thinks they're better than proprietary licenses).</p><p>By default, open licenses help the powerful more than they help the powerless. Why? Because they're applied to technology. And technology multiplies potential, it doesn't add it.</p><p>To develop technology that's disproportionately advantageous to \"the little guy\", you need to design that in, not just open license something and hope for the best.</p>",
